    stem: "Olivia and Jim were reseeding 20% of their grassland for their mixed beef and dairy farm. The paper prints a two-column table headed Types / Reason with three numbered rows to fill in.",
    questionText: "List with reason three types of plants they could include in the mixture.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"3x (3+1)"}, totalMarks: 12,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "alt", verbatim: "Perennial ryegrass — Palatability", marks: 4, accepts: ["productivity","digestibility"], contextNote: "Each row is one plant-and-reason pair worth 4: 3 marks for naming the plant, 1 for a reason that matches THAT plant. Scheme pairings — perennial or Italian ryegrass: palatability, productivity, digestibility; clover: high protein content, fixes N, reduces the need for artificial fertiliser, palatability; chicory: high protein content, anthelminthic, palatable; plantain: anthelminthic properties, drought resistant, deep rooting. Italian ryegrass scores in place of perennial ryegrass on this row." },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "alt", verbatim: "Clover — High protein content", marks: 4, accepts: ["fixes N","palatability"], contextNote: "3 for the plant, 1 for its matching reason. Clover's other accepted reasons are fixes N, palatability, and reduces the need for artificial fertiliser (that last one is split across the marks column in the PDF extraction, so it is named here rather than in accepts)." },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "alt", verbatim: "Chicory — anthelminthic", marks: 4, accepts: ["High protein content","palatable"], contextNote: "3 for the plant, 1 for its matching reason. Four plants are on offer and only three are claimed, so plantain scores in place of any of the three worked here — its reasons are anthelminthic properties, drought resistant, deep rooting." },
    ],
